<section class="history" id="history">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="history-header">
      <p class="section-label">History</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">The Making of a Prairie Masterwork</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="history-body">
      <div class="history-text">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          When Sherman and Elizabeth Booth set out to find land north of Chicago in the years before
          the First World War, they were searching for something that most Chicagoans could only
          imagine: a large wooded parcel between two ravines, where a gentleman farmer might build
          a home worthy of the landscape. They found it in Glencoe — a dramatic site nestled between
          deep ravines and the Chicago &amp; Northwestern Railroad right of way — and they found the
          architect to match it in Oak Park, where the houses of Frank Lloyd Wright had already begun
          to transform American domestic life.
        </p>

        <p>
          What followed was one of the most richly layered commissions of Wright's Prairie period.
          The Booths envisioned not just a house but an entire residential enclave — the Ravine
          Bluffs Development — with Wright designing the principal house, five smaller homes for
          neighboring lots, a concrete bridge spanning the ravine, and three sculptural entrance
          markers in poured concrete. Correspondence as early as January 1910 references the Booth
          project; by 1911, Wright had produced the first full design. Scheme I called for an
          elaborate estate priced at $125,000 — more than three times Booth's budget of $30–35,000.
          The design was scaled back to what became Scheme II.
        </p>

        <p>
          The house as built incorporates an existing stable and gardener's cottage already on the
          site, adapted and folded into a new main core containing the living room and north porch.
          From there Wright stacked a second floor of bedrooms with a cantilevered balcony, a
          third-floor sleeping porch with a roof garden above, and an open tower over the central
          stairwell. The result is a structure of unusual vertical ambition for a Prairie house —
          rising four stories from the ravine floor, with stucco-on-metal-lath walls and the
          characteristic deep-shadowed eaves of Wright's mature domestic work.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          "There was a swinging footbridge across the ravine to the 'farming area.'"
          <cite>— Sherman Booth III, recalling childhood at 265 Sylvan Road</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="history-subhead">The Architecture</h3>

        <p>
          The Booth House is a Prairie design that defies the style's usual horizontality. Where
          most Wright Prairie houses hug the earth, this one climbs — and it does so with a
          disciplined square geometry that runs through every detail. The door knobs are square. The
          sconces are square. The art glass, the built-in bookshelves, and the window hardware all
          carry the same angular precision. Wright called this kind of total integration the
          "grammar" of the house: once a geometric theme was established, it governed everything
          from the structural bays down to the smallest piece of hardware.
        </p>

        <p>
          The interior is organized around a grand living room measuring 22 by 36 feet — generous
          even by today's standards — with an all-season enclosed porch to the north overlooking
          the ravine and gardens below. Four fireplaces are distributed through the house; the most
          dramatic is on the rooftop deck, where an outdoor brick fireplace commands a view of the
          Lake Shore Country Club and the woodland ravine that wraps the property. The master
          bedroom features Wright-designed lantern fixtures and a run of windows that wrap a corner
          of the house — a detail that speaks to Wright's lifelong preoccupation with the corner as
          a problem to be dissolved rather than emphasized.
        </p>

        <h3 class="history-subhead">A Total Work of Art</h3>

        <p>
          Wright designed the house as a <em>Gesamtkunstwerk</em> — a total work of art. Beyond
          the architecture, he created all the furniture, light fixtures, and hardware as part of
          the commission: five twin-sized beds, a dining table, two host chairs, eight side chairs,
          and a dressing table. The original light fixtures survive in place throughout the house,
          as do most of the door hardware and window fittings and two of the five Wright-designed
          beds — making the Booth House one of the most intact ensembles of Wright-designed
          furnishings in existence. When the Ravine Bluffs Development was listed on the National
          Register of Historic Places in 1978, the Booth House was recognized as its defining
          centerpiece.
        </p>

        <h3 class="history-subhead">Later Stewardship</h3>

        <p>
          The house remained in the Booth family until August 1958, when Elizabeth Booth sold it
          to Julius and Elinor Abler at a price of $38,500. The Ablers subsequently subdivided the
          property and transferred the house to Northwestern University. In 1967, Theodore and Sonia
          Bloch purchased the property, beginning a decades-long stewardship that would define the
          house's modern life. The Blochs undertook careful structural and cosmetic restoration,
          removing a deteriorated garage, and — working with Chicago architect Masami Takayama —
          creating a new carport that incorporates Wright's original decorative screens. The house
          continues today as a private residence and one of the finest surviving examples of
          Wright's Prairie domestic architecture.
        </p>
      </div>

